{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And when He gives quiet, who can condemn?</b> — [The hiphil of] ישקיט is in the sense of motion (נצוח), and like it: 'and wherever he turns he condemns' (ובכל אשר יפנה ירשיע). The meaning is the opposite of 'gives quiet,' for at the time of motion (נצוח) human beings are stirred — do you not see? He says: 'and the wicked are like the tossing sea' (והרשעים כים נגרש), for it cannot be still." "<b>And He hides His face</b> — the opposite of 'gives quiet'; then all the wars are aroused." "<b>Over a nation</b> — over a people, and also over a single man alone. And He does not enthrone a wicked man (חנף) except [as] a snare for the people, in order to take vengeance upon them."], :book :job, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "The root נצח/נצוח is read here as related to continuous motion or surging, contrasting with שקט (stillness); the Hebrew is compressed and the argument stays cryptic."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Job -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/eyov/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:04:32.451860103Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}